# Historic Structure Report: The McClean Barn, Gettysburg National Military Park (Kenneth W. Holt, P.E., and John M. Dickey, F.A.I.A., for the National Park Service; July 1985)
# A structural report on the barn as it stood in 1985. Its history chapter (printed pp. 9-40) dates the parts of the barn; its recommendations say what of 1863 the barn keeps. Statements about additions of c. 1869, c. 1880 and c. 1890 are later features (never drawn in 1863).
# The default text variant of this source is the content-stream-order text (pdftotext -raw): the standard text interleaves the lines of the wide text frames. Printed page numbers are the numbers at the foot of the page.
@ NPS-HSR-MCCLEAN-BARN

12/8 | buildings | The McClean barn was relatively new in 1863 | - | 1863 | stated | The report says the McClean barn was relatively new in 1863 and represents a number of regional characteristics of the classic period of barn design | #s2
30/26 | buildings | The frame barn standing in 1863 was built about 1854 on the site of an earlier log barn | - | 1863 (the barn of about 1854; the chronology is the report's own suggestion) | hedged | The report suggests the chronology: a log barn in the same location built after 1798, a frame barn incorporating the north wall built about 1854, and says that the stone for the foundations did not come from a quarry on the place | #s5
30/26 | buildings | Additions to the McClean barn: north about 1869, east about 1880, south about 1890, all there in 1907, demolished in 1944 | - | !1869-1890 (additions after the battle; demolished 1944) | hedged | The report suggests that the north addition dates from about 1869, the east addition from about 1880 and the south addition from about 1890, all there in 1907, and that the additions were demolished and the west wall underpinned in 1944 | #s5
44/41 | buildings | The barn has the historic shape it had in 1863 except ventilators, siding and louvers | - | 1863 (shape of the barn; the report calls it unchanged except for a few details) | stated | The report says that, as Marcella Sherfy points out, the barn now has the historic shape it had in 1863 and, except for a few details like ventilators, siding and louvers, the visitor's impression is much the same as that of the soldiers | #s3
44/41 | hospitals | No evidence that the McClean barn or house was used as a hospital or quarters; the tenant had joined the Union army and his family left before Rodes's infantry appeared | - | 1863-07-01 | stated | The report says there is no evidence that the barn or the house was used as hospital or living quarters during the battle, that the tenant farmer had joined the Union Army some time before July and his family had moved out of the house before Rodes's infantry appeared on Oak Ridge about 2:30 in the afternoon of the first day | #s4-5
44/41 | damage | Judge McClean's later claim: buildings injured, fences and crops destroyed; no damage to the buildings described, so probably minimal | - | 1863-07 (a claim made later) | hedged | The report says that in his later claim for damages Judge McClean said that the buildings had been injured and fences and crops destroyed but described no damage to the buildings, so it is reasonable to assume it was minimal | #s6
53/50 | buildings | The original siding of 1863 remains on the south gable: random-width tongue-and-groove pine boards about 12 inches wide and 1 inch thick, circular sawn, painted red outside | - | 1863 | stated | The report says the original siding which covered the walls in 1863 still remains on the south gable, of random-width tongue and groove pine boards averaging 12 inches wide and exactly 1 inch thick, circular sawn, unpainted on the inside and painted red, about the same color that now exists, on the outside | #s1-3
53/50 | buildings | Paint history: red primer and paint not renewed for many years; 1880s photographs show a dark gable and white louvers; two or more coats of white under the present red | - | ~1880s (later paint states of the barn; the original red is stated for 1863 in the preceding record) | stated | The report says the original red paint was also used as a primer and was not painted again for many years, that photographs from the 1880s show the south gable end as dark in contrast to the white louvers, and that under the present red paint are two or more coats of white paint over isolated fragments of whitewash | #s4-7
29/25 | buildings | A photograph of 1885 shows that the south wall was originally stone | - | ~1885 (photograph of the barn) | stated | The report's caption says a photograph of 1885 indicates that the south wall of the barn was originally stone | #s1
31/27 | buildings | The mid-nineteenth century photograph appears to show a stone south wall; whether the east wall under the forebay was stone in 1863 cannot be told | - | 1863 (stone walls of the lower level; the report is unsure of the south and east walls) | hedged | The report says the north and west walls of the lower level were stone masonry, that the mid-nineteenth century photograph of the south wall appears to show a stone wall, and that because the east wall under the forebay was always in shadow it is impossible to tell whether that had been stone masonry at the time of the battle | #s6-9
19/15 | buildings | Plan of the McClean barn: two barn floors side by side between two mows; the north door and north mow three feet wider than the south, over the equipment bay and corn crib | - | pre-1863 (original fabric of the barn of about 1854 as the report describes it) | stated | The report says the barn's plan includes two barn floors side by side between two mows with two big doors to the ramp, and that the north door and the north mow, which extends over the equipment bay and corn crib, are about three feet wider than the south door and mow | #s3-5
13/9 | buildings | The barn has an equipment storage bay in the lower level at the north end and a corn crib ventilated on two sides | - | pre-1863 (original design of the barn of about 1854 as the report describes it) | stated | The report says that, like most Pennsylvania barns built after 1825, the barn has an equipment storage bay in the lower level at the north end, which also allowed the corn crib to be ventilated on two sides | #s7-8
27/23 | buildings | The west stone wall of the barn rebuilt on a new concrete footing dated 1944 | - | !1944 (rebuilt west wall; the additions torn down about then; sold to the park association in 1965) | stated | The report says the west stone wall has obviously been rebuilt on a new concrete footing bearing the inscribed date of 1944, when the owner presumably tore down the additions, planning to sell to the Gettysburg Battlefield Preservation Association, which finally happened in 1965 | #s7
